===Jefferson nickels (1938–present)===
{{Main|Jefferson nickel}}

Jefferson nickels have been minted since 1938 at the Philadelphia and Denver mints and from the San Francisco mint until 1970. Key dates for the series include the 1939-D, and 1950-D nickels. The 1939-D nickel with a mintage of 3,514,000 coins is the second lowest behind the 1950-D nickel. The cause of the key date of 1939 stems from the new design that excited collectors the year prior, after the initial hype had settled down fewer nickels were saved.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bellaonline.com/articles/art178490.asp|title=1939-D Jefferson Nickel Now A Key Date|work=www.bellaonline.com|access-date=January 15, 2015}}</ref> 2,630,000 nickels were minted in Denver in 1950, this remains the lowest mintage for the Denver mint in the series. Despite its low mintage the nickel is not rare, its value is thought to be connected to the [[Brilliant Uncirculated|brilliant uncirculated]] [[coin roll|roll]] boom that burst between 1963 and 1964.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.coinweek.com/us-coins/us-coins-75-years-jefferson-nickels/|title=US Coins: 75 Years of Jefferson Nickels|work=www.coinweek.com|date=September 19, 2013|access-date=January 15, 2015}}</ref> On the opposite spectrum, the year 1964 saw the largest combined mintage of nickels to date. The result of the large mintages were due to a widespread shortage of small change that was blamed on coin collectors.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.numismaster.com/ta/numis/Article.jsp?ad=article&ArticleId=27140|title=1964 a Momentous Year in Coins|work=www.numismaster.com|date=August 19, 2013|access-date=January 16, 2015}}</ref> the following year, The [[Coinage Act of 1965]] removed all mint marks from nickels that were issued by the mints, this lasted until 1968 when the mintmark was moved from the reverse to the obverse side of the coin.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usmint.gov/about_the_mint/collectors_corner/?action=mint_marks|title=Why Mint Marks?|work=www.usmint.gov|access-date=January 16, 2015}}</ref>

